\section*{The exact reconstruction threshold of the ferromagnetic four-state channel}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. Four-state ferromagnetic broadcasting is claimed to have exact threshold $d\lambda^2>1$ on regular and observed Poisson trees, with no reconstruction at equality.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} For the ferromagnetic four-state channel \(P_\lambda(j|i)=\lambda\mathbf1_{i=j}+(1-\lambda)/4\), \(0\le\lambda\le1\), let
\[
 a_n=\mathbb E\left[\tfrac12\sum_{i=1}^4
 |\mathbb P(\sigma_\rho=i\mid T,\sigma_{L_n})-1/4|\right].
\]
The source claims \(d\lambda^2\le1\Rightarrow a_n\to0\), both on every rooted integer \(d\)-ary tree with \(d\ge2\) and on Poisson-\(d\) Galton--Watson trees for every real \(d>0\). The random-tree expectation is not conditioned on survival, and the entire tree is observed. Equality is included. Combined with the usual positive-side reconstruction criterion, this identifies the strict threshold.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Symmetric posterior experiments satisfy a preserved algebraic constraint. Polynomial inequalities give a strict information saving under branch combination, and reproducible rational-arithmetic certificates verify the inequalities; iteration forces posterior information to zero at and below the boundary.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Four-state ferromagnetic broadcasting is claimed to have exact threshold $d\lambda^2>1$ on regular and observed Poisson trees, with no reconstruction at equality. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 229, the three-state Comparator entries only establish reconstruction when $d\lambda^2>1$. They omit threshold nonreconstruction, the graph recovery corollary, and the four-state companions. No Comparator is assigned to the selected manuscript here; the family page selects other papers or supporting statements. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

